    {
      "id": "vblA",
      "code_hash": "f69f52c8964e9bf83fdef6239b0532d2",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"4-contracts\">4. Contracts</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Say a colleague's transformation feeds the billing job you own. Its\ndocstring promises the new column is never negative and never NULL, and\ncustomers are invoiced on that promise. You'd rather it were proved than\npromised.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">In verified programming you state what a function <strong>requires</strong> of its\ninputs (preconditions) and what it <strong>ensures</strong> about its output\n(postconditions), and the machine proves the second follows from the\nfirst. <code>sverify</code> brings that to data transformations: a function from\nIbis tables to an Ibis table, with a decorator that states the contract.\nWe'll write one.</span>\n<h2 id=\"a-first-contract\">A first contract</h2></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "bkHC",
      "code_hash": "6c0d95f2b5c12a73aef8ef8f8a5a5297",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/html": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>postcondition</th>\n<th>status</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>(_.doubled &gt;= 0)</code></td>\n<td>\u2705 proved</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>_.doubled.notnull()</code></td>\n<td>\u2705 proved</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>id IS NOT NULL</code></td>\n<td>\u2705 proved</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>runtime</td>\n<td>\u26a0\ufe0f may wrap around: ($2 * 2)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>output column</th>\n<th>known values</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>id</code></td>\n<td>any integer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>doubled</code></td>\n<td>an integer \u2265 22</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "lEQa",
      "code_hash": "332c6670f9e018a15f182a0c8f362b61",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><ul>\n<li><code>requires</code> maps each <strong>parameter</strong> of the function to conditions on\n  that input. <code>ensures</code> lists conditions on the output.</li>\n<li>Conditions are Ibis expressions over the table's columns (<code>_.amount &gt;= 0</code>,\n  using Ibis's <code>_</code> placeholder, or a function of the table) or SQL\n  snippets (<code>\"id IS NOT NULL\"</code>).</li>\n<li>Calling <code>doubled(orders)</code> builds the Ibis expression as usual. Before\n  returning it, <code>sverify</code> sends the plan and the conditions to the Lean\n  checker (<code>substrait-check --contract</code>), which tries to <strong>prove</strong> every\n  postcondition. The verdicts are in <code>.last_report</code>.</li>\n<li>The first table of the report has the verdicts. The second is\n  everything Lean established about each output column, whether you\n  asked or not: a free, proved data dictionary.</li>\n<li>The \u26a0\ufe0f row says <code>amount * 2</code> may leave the 64-bit range and wrap around,\n  since nothing bounds <code>amount</code> from above. Chapter 6 is about these.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><code>sv.verify(expr, requires=..., ensures=...)</code> does the same for an\nexpression without a decorator; it returns the report instead of raising.</span>\n<h2 id=\"how-does-lean-know-doubled-22\">How does Lean know <code>doubled \u2265 22</code>?</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Lean never sees a single order. It runs the plan on <em>descriptions</em> of\nvalues instead of values: for each column, whether it can be NULL and\nwhich values it can hold. This is called <em>abstract interpretation</em>.\nEach operator updates the descriptions:</span>\n<ul>\n<li>the precondition says <code>amount &gt;= 0</code> is TRUE on every input row, so\n  <code>amount</code> is a non-NULL integer \u2265 0 (<code>NULL &gt;= 0</code> is NULL, not TRUE);</li>\n<li>the filter <code>amount &gt; 10</code> keeps only rows where that is TRUE, so <code>amount</code>\n  is a non-NULL integer \u2265 11 afterwards (NULL &gt; 10 is not TRUE);</li>\n<li><code>amount * 2</code> is then an integer \u2265 22, and never NULL.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">A postcondition is proved when its description says it can only be TRUE.\nChange the transformation and the conditions below, and we'll watch\nwhat Lean can establish:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
